{"id": "a121a373ce62", "lang": "peo", "word": "𐎱𐎡𐎫𐎠", "created": "2026-11-08T23:59:00Z", "start": {"stage": "ine-pro", "name": "Proto-Indo-European", "form": "ph₂tḗr"}, "end": {"form": "pitˈār", "recorded": "𐎱𐎡𐎫𐎠"}, "disabled": [], "enabled": ["peo~pal:r_stem_oblique"], "levels": [{"stage": "ine-pro", "form": "ph₂tḗr", "name": "Proto-Indo-European"}, {"stage": "iir-pro", "form": "patˈār", "name": "Proto-Indo-Iranian"}, {"stage": "ira-pro", "form": "patˈār", "name": "Proto-Iranian"}, {"stage": "peo", "form": "pitˈār", "name": "Old Persian"}, {"stage": "pal", "form": "pidˈar", "name": "Middle Persian"}, {"stage": "fa", "form": "pedar", "name": "Persian"}], "laws": [{"step": "ine-pro→iir-pro", "id": "syllabic_laryngeals", "name": "Syllabic laryngeals become vowels", "tier": "established"}, {"step": "ine-pro→iir-pro", "id": "long_vowel_merger", "name": "*ē and *ō merge as *ā", "tier": "established"}, {"step": "ira-pro→peo", "id": "anaptyxis_pat", "name": "OP *i for the laryngeal vowel of *patā́-", "tier": "proposed"}, {"step": "peo→pal", "id": "r_stem_oblique", "name": "The r-stem kinship nouns continue their oblique stem: *-tār > MP -tar", "tier": "custom"}, {"step": "peo→pal", "id": "t_to_d", "name": "Intervocalic *-t- > MP *-d-", "tier": "established"}, {"step": "pal→fa", "id": "i_to_e", "name": "MP *i > NP e", "tier": "proposed"}, {"step": "pal→fa", "id": "stress_not_written", "name": "Stress is not written", "tier": "established"}], "note": "𐎱𐎡𐎫𐎠 pitā is an Old Persian r-stem: the nominative in -ā is not the vowel Middle Persian continues (pidar), which has the short -a of the oblique stem, and New Persian pedar follows from it by the ordinary laws. The paradigm rule r_stem_oblique states that replacement and is switched on here so the timeline carried into Middle Persian and Persian shows it; it stays off for every other word, because the agent-noun -tār (NP dādār) keeps its long ā.", "references": ["kent-1953", "mackenzie-1971", "wiktionary"], "default": true}
